00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.070
then maybe share a little
bit about your role,

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.250
and also something you
like to do outside of work.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:28.760
Sure.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.090
Thanks, Rachael, and thanks
for having me here today.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.290
I was super excited about
it when you reached out.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.820
I was thrilled.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.410
I love talking about this stuff.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.010
So as Rachael
mentioned, I'm Director

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.750
of Analytics at MarketBridge.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:46.320
We're a-- call ourselves
go-to-market science company,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.720
and so we really use
reproducible scientific methods

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.950
to tackle our clients'
toughest measurement problems

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.350
in sales and marketing.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.400
My role is--

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:05.170
I wear a lot of hats, but
I guess, primarily, I'm

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:11.160
focused on our tooling
and our approach

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.620
to building tools
that we can apply

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.900
across different
problems, as well as some

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.470
of the front-end
stuff we do with Shiny

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.247
and serving up
insights to clients

00:03:21.247 --> 00:03:22.455
based on the models we build.

00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:27.250
I'm sure we'll get into more
questions about some of that,

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:28.980
but I'm from Cleveland, Ohio.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.490
I live right outside of
Cleveland, and outside of work,

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.800
I love playing basketball.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:37.400
Awesome.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.930
Thank you, Dan.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.590
So to kind of put us in
your mindset of the problems

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:44.840
that you're helping
to solve, what's

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.000
an example of a marketing
use case or maybe a problem

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.680
you've helped the
stakeholder with?

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.240
Sure.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:57.230
Probably the most common
problem that we work on

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.800
that every company
is challenged with

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.660
is measuring their
marketing effectiveness.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.240
Or you think about all
the different efforts

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.490
you do from a marketing
perspective-- reaching out

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:09.770
to customers directly
through different channels,

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:14.330
direct mail, email, phone,
spending money on paid ads,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.535
on various social media
channels, and paid search,

00:04:17.535 --> 00:04:18.660
and all that kind of stuff.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:24.350
So we help our clients measure
those efforts effectively

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:29.960
and in a way that doesn't
double count or overattribute.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.870
So we've got some
very smart approaches

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.410
to these sorts of problems
and some very creative ways

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.270
to address them.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.650
So maybe these
could be related,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.140
but I know on past
Hangouts, we've

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.350
talked a lot about community,
and so, for example,

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.020
like an internal
meetup and being

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.600
able to show the value of that
within your own organization.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.670
Do you have some
tips for people here

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:03.070
who might be trying to do that?

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:08.560
Well, I would say,
without getting too

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.590
into the weeds in
some of this stuff,

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.560
try to think of the
problems in more broad ways.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.770
It's like, what is your goal?

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:20.810
Like, is your goal to--

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.510
by having internal meetups,
is your goal to get-- like,

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:24.500
what's your objective?

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.300
What's your measurable
objective on the back end?

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.680
Are you trying to
get more people

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:30.920
to register for these
events going forward?

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:35.810
Or is it more of a
softer lead type of thing

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:37.700
to get people to sign
up for the conference,

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.040
and just using
Posit as an example.

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:44.390
But think about the
problem holistically

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:47.528
before you start diving
into polling data

00:05:47.528 --> 00:05:49.070
and arranging data
and all that stuff

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.110
because the more you
can think about it--

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:54.020
the bigger picture of what's
going on, the more you'll

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.820
be able to--

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.100
want to explain what you're
doing to other people,

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:04.040
but also think about how all
the different pieces that

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.400
affect what you're trying to
measure can come into play

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.020
and work together
with each other.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:14.100
Thank you.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.780
So I get to ask you
all the questions

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:18.750
right in the beginning, as
we're thinking of the ones--

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.440
as people are
thinking of their own.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.780
But I know you said
you use Shiny a bit,

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.510
so I'd love to hear
a little bit more

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:26.970
about how you're
using Shiny today.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.770
Sure.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.320
So, first of all,
I'm a huge fan,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.600
and I've been
following the product.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:36.780
It's called Product Along
since it first launched,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.812
and I knew it was
going to be a game

00:06:38.812 --> 00:06:40.770
changer because, at the
time, you could already

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.560
do some really incredible
stuff visually with R.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:49.590
And the moment I saw how the
reactive framework worked

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.930
and how to manipulate
things and have

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:53.783
it change what users
were looking at,

00:06:53.783 --> 00:06:55.200
I knew it was going
to be awesome.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.970
So I've spent a lot of time in
the last seven or eight years--

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.143
can't remember how
long it's been--

00:07:02.143 --> 00:07:03.810
trying to get better
and trying to learn

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:06.690
the ins and outs of Shiny.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:10.800
But at the end of the
day, I look at it like--

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.390
your ability to create in a tool
like ours is pretty limitless.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.070
Like, there are so many--

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:19.533
like, I compare it
to LEGOs, right?

00:07:19.533 --> 00:07:21.450
There's all sorts of
cool LEGO sets out there.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.160
Think of a package
maybe as a LEGO set.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.380
And the fact that you can just
pull from different packages,

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.940
and you can kind of create
your own original LEGO

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.790
creation from all
these different,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.905
really strong and stable
parts that come underneath it.

00:07:35.905 --> 00:07:38.280
So as soon as you kind of
accept that, and then you think

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.420
how Shiny can take
that to the next level,

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:45.570
you're kind of giving your users
the ability to take your LEGOs

00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:47.520
and rearrange them
in different ways

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.110
and do some cool stuff with it.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:55.170
So now to talk a little bit more
about actual work-related stuff

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.390
and not LEGOs, but with our--

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.250
I like the LEGOs explanation.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:00.390
That's helpful.

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.660
Yeah, but with what we do--
think about building marketing

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.180
measurement or
marketing mix models.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:09.870
When we can serve
those up to our clients

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:13.330
and have them change
the data around

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:16.060
that they are scoring
those models on-- maybe

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:17.950
they're making
assumptions around

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:20.290
how they might spend
differently next quarter

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:21.340
on a certain channel.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:23.602
Or maybe their budget's
going to be reduced,

00:08:23.602 --> 00:08:25.060
and they need to
scale things back.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.910
They can rescore the
models, and all that's

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:31.150
happening on the
reactive back end.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.480
They're using some inputs
to change assumptions

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.990
around the data and then
having the models get rescored.

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.590
And, oh, someone pointed
something about Duplos, yeah.

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:41.530
[LAUGHS]

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:43.450
But, yeah, that's a lot of it--

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:47.680
seeing visuals get
regenerated that show time

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:54.700
series plots or tables that
have some way to summarize data.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.800
That's a lot of what
we're doing, manipulating

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.100
those models on the back end
and restoring them and showing

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:01.360
results in different ways.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:04.680
Awesome.

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:06.910
Alan, I see you just asked
a question in the chat.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.340
Do you want to jump in here?

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:09.000
Yeah, sure.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:09.900
Hi, Dan.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.820
I've been learning a
little bit about some

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:14.340
of what our teams are doing
with marketing effectiveness

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:16.810
measurement lately, and
it's an interesting space,

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.390
and there's a ton of
challenges and stuff to learn.

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:23.250
So this is timely and fun to get
to learn from you a little bit.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.620
I'm curious if you can talk
a little bit about your team

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.000
and what the roles are there.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.840
And maybe say a little bit
more about, as a director,

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.400
are you leading people,
or is your leadership

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.430
in a more of a kind of
technical space or a mix,

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:37.860
and how you find that balance?

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.600
Sure, great question.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.670
So my role is in a little bit
more of a technical space.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:48.850
We do have a good structure
to our team, though, I think.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.430
It's a little tough to
describe because it's

00:09:53.430 --> 00:10:00.420
a mixture of sort of flat
but also with specialty.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.210
So we know we have-- and
this is an important thing

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.880
I was hoping to bring up at
some point in this conversation.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:11.930
But where I've seen
teams fail or not

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:16.190
succeed as well is when
people are trying to--

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:19.100
there's a single role that
leadership has in mind,

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.150
and everyone has to
fit into that role.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:23.150
And that's not the
case that we have here.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.612
We have people that are
really good with theoretical

00:10:26.612 --> 00:10:28.070
statistics, and we
have people that

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:32.360
are really good with data
storage and data storage

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:35.390
solutions and data
engineering, I guess.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:37.250
We have people that
are good with Shiny,

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:39.320
and we have people good
with data visualization.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:45.060
So what we try to do is we-- it
involves a lot of communication

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.180
too and breaking projects down
into individual components,

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:52.380
where we can say who fits
best on those components

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:56.280
so that we can get the best out
of everybody while also giving

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:58.702
exposure to different
components so that people

00:10:58.702 --> 00:10:59.910
can learn in different areas.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.190
But recognizing
those differences

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:07.710
and trying to get the most
out of people's expertise

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:08.535
is huge for that.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:16.110
I see there was an
anonymous question.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.260
It goes back to us
talking about Shiny,

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:22.040
and it said, "Do you think that
the main value that R provides

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:22.670
is Shiny?"

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.380
And this person
said, "Personally, I

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:29.360
find the overemphasis
on Shiny sometimes

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:31.730
makes R seem like a
limited language."

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:35.418
And I was curious to get
your thoughts on that.

00:11:35.418 --> 00:11:36.710
Yeah, that's a good question.

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:39.370
It's a good point.

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:43.030
I don't see it that
way because I've

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:48.400
been using R for a long time
and have seen it evolve.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:51.550
So, obviously, I think--
it wasn't by much,

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:55.150
but I believe R Markdown
preceded the release of Shiny.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:58.360
So that was pretty
innovative at the time.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:01.840
You could create reports in
all sorts of different formats

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:03.310
and embed output and code.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:06.650
And so that is still
a great use case.

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.590
In fact, that's
one of the things

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:10.210
when I'm talking to clients.

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.670
And in best-case
scenario, we have

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:15.400
clients that can log
into our environment

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.900
and see some of the cool stuff
we create in our environment.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.230
But there are other
cases when, just by--

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:22.730
I'm sure everybody on the phone
here has been in a situation

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:25.580
where they've had to work in a
different computing environment

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.290
without all the
resources that they would

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:29.100
love to have at their disposal.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.160
So we find ourselves in
that situation sometimes,

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:35.720
and when we're fortunate
enough to have even the ability

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:38.900
to install RStudio
Desktop or something,

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:41.210
your ability to create
those types of reports

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:42.870
now becomes on the table.

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:45.230
And one thing that I
think gets overlooked

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:49.010
a lot is all the really
cool and somewhat

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:53.020
interactive visualizations you
can do in R Markdown output.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:57.320
But I'm a huge fan of those HTML
widgets collection of packages,

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.950
so like DT for tables
and Leaflet for maps,

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.890
and dygraphs for time series
plots, and things like that.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:07.693
A lot of clients and
companies you might work with

00:13:07.693 --> 00:13:09.860
may have never even seen
something like that before.

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:12.170
They might not
even know that they

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:17.690
could get a static document that
has an embedded map with icons

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:19.730
for individual
lat/long coordinates,

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:22.280
and polygons for
shapes, and they

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:24.890
have hover over to see
different information

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:27.010
at different regions.

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:31.310
So those have gone
a long way, right?

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:33.300
I mean, they've gone a
long way with clients.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:36.800
They've been received very
well, and that obviously

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:39.890
stopped short of
Shiny reactivity.

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:44.930
But then even just as an
exploratory or language

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:47.960
to explore data, manipulate
data, generate plots

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:51.050
in the Viewer, even pasting
those into PowerPoint,

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:54.200
even if they're static
plots, can go a long way,

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.110
and, of course, building
models and generating

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:03.080
insights around inferential
statistics as well.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.240
Absolutely.

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:07.692
I think also we talk
about Shiny apps

00:14:07.692 --> 00:14:09.400
a lot and the
visualizations because it's

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:12.820
fun to talk about the
things that are created

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:16.450
and things that people are
actually using and interacting

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:18.010
with.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.890
A question-- I'm not sure if
I'm going to frame this question

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:25.440
the right way or if I fully
thought about it, but I--

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:27.750
so I read the book,
Checklist Manifesto,

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:31.200
and I've been thinking
about if there

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:36.540
may be some sort of
checklist for providing value

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:42.270
to an organization or a way
for your team to best impact

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.263
the business with data science.

00:14:44.263 --> 00:14:45.930
And I was wondering
if there's something

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:50.310
that comes to mind for you,
like something that you say,

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:53.558
oh, you definitely need to
have this to provide impact

00:14:53.558 --> 00:14:54.225
on the business.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.005
Let me think about that
because there was a little bit

00:15:00.005 --> 00:15:00.505
[INAUDIBLE].

00:15:00.505 --> 00:15:06.920
Yeah, I'm still forming what
I want that question to be.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:11.600
Like, a tip for all of us
on impacting the business

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:12.860
with data science.

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:14.240
Mhm, mhm.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:23.550
So I think every business
is going to have KPIs,

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:25.920
and there's going to be
other metrics besides KPIs,

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:28.390
things that lead into that.

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:32.930
And as crazy as it sounds,
some organizations struggle

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:38.000
to measure those and to do so
in a consistent and repeatable

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.060
way, you know?

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.480
So maybe they measure
something that

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:44.717
just comes from one
person sitting at a desk,

00:15:44.717 --> 00:15:46.800
and they've done it for
six years, and they leave,

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:48.910
and all of a sudden, who
knows how they do that?

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.480
So just getting in a--

00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:54.930
creating a framework for
consistent measurement

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:57.970
is huge for an
organization, right?

00:15:57.970 --> 00:16:00.030
The measurement is consistent.

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.580
The outcomes are
measured consistently.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:06.180
Then taking action to
improve those outcomes

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.640
can be thought of
as more reliable

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:11.670
because the measurement
process is consistent.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:13.875
So that would be
one thing for sure.

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:20.440
And I guess another--

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:25.710
on that note, it's
decisioning, right?

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.560
Every company makes
decisions in there.

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:32.220
A lot of us are here because we
like to do this kind of work,

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.830
but most of our companies
exist because they

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.630
like to make money,
and they like to grow.

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:41.940
And so we find a
balance between doing

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:44.770
what we do to help our company
to achieve their goals.

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:51.210
And so if you can find ways to
help your company optimize cost

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:55.290
and reduce waste and
increase growth--

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:58.237
and, again, all of that
is through measuring

00:16:58.237 --> 00:17:00.570
and looking at decisions that
have been made in the past

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:02.570
and thinking about how
they could have been made

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:04.800
differently and doing
historical analysis or building

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:08.010
models to help make
those decisions

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:09.900
more effectively--
then that's obviously

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:13.930
a huge win for any organization.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:15.700
Thank you.

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:18.280
Josh, I see you had asked
a question in the chat.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:19.030
Do you to jump in?

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:21.280
And maybe introduce
yourself because you'll

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:24.589
be joining us as a featured
leader in a few weeks here too.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:25.089
Sure.

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:26.119
Thanks, Rachael.

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:29.050
Yeah, so I'm Josh King.

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:32.180
I think, April 20, I'll be a
[AUDIO OUT] leader as well.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:34.030
And similar to you,
Dan, I focus a lot

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:35.410
on marketing data science.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:38.650
One of the things that really
caught my ear earlier--

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:42.040
you referencing you
have a Shiny framework,

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:44.740
and you reference specifically
MMM as an input to that

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:46.360
and being able to--
for your clients

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:49.220
to use that as a scoring
mechanism for new models.

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:52.870
So I'm curious to try to give
some thought to this myself

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:53.950
in the past.

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:57.520
Are you using that as a means
for actually constructing

00:17:57.520 --> 00:17:59.020
MMM for your clients?

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:02.400
Or is it more of a
scenario-planning aspect

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:06.220
to utilize response curves
coming out of MMM for them

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:08.060
to actually use it
as a tool for them?

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:11.620
And the reason that I've had
a lot of struggle wrapping

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.900
my head around
this in the past is

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:17.810
I see MMM not necessarily as
a specific model, necessarily,

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.960
but more of a
framework to build out

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.090
what it is that you're
trying to measure, which

00:18:22.090 --> 00:18:23.548
is very human in
the loop, which is

00:18:23.548 --> 00:18:26.048
kind of different from a lot
of other data science projects.

00:18:26.048 --> 00:18:28.390
It's very much a blend of
the art and the science

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:32.170
of having a balance
between statistical fit,

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:33.280
as well as business fit.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:36.250
So I'm curious, within the the
apps you've built in the past,

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.420
like how you're able
to find that balance.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:42.280
Yeah, great question,
and nice to meet you, Josh.

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:46.150
It's almost like you've spoken
to some of our leaders here,

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:50.440
and they planted that question
with you, but we are--

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:54.025
currently, I would say the
state is more of the latter

00:18:54.025 --> 00:18:57.790
that you mentioned, taking
models that our team has worked

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:59.800
very hard to build
and very precise

00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:03.310
and serving those up in a way
to give clients the ability

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:05.560
to understand how
those models work,

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:08.830
and consider different scenarios
where they think about how they

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:11.920
might manipulate
spend differently,

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:15.290
or what would happen if this
happens sort of a thing.

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:17.620
But totally agree on your
point about making it

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:21.280
more of a fluid
application, where

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.650
it's not just reacting to
something that's been built,

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:28.010
but also has a hand in
building it to begin with.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:30.708
So that's definitely in mind
and on our roadmap for how

00:19:30.708 --> 00:19:31.750
we approach these things.

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:37.620
It's just it's actually one
of the more exciting things

00:19:37.620 --> 00:19:40.520
that I think about that I'm
working on this year, so

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:41.145
great question.

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:48.070
So we were double-checking
in the chat on what MMM stands

00:19:48.070 --> 00:19:50.740
for and thought it might
be helpful to include with

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:52.960
the recording too.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:53.950
So what is MMM?

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:54.987
[LAUGHS]

00:19:54.987 --> 00:19:57.070
So you might hear different
answers from people,

00:19:57.070 --> 00:20:00.070
but Marketing Mix Models
or Media Mix Models.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.650
But, basically,
when you look at--

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:04.180
you consider all
the different types

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:06.070
of marketing
stimulus or marketing

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:08.830
efforts that go towards
a measurable objective.

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:16.670
And it's trying to consider all
those things at the same time

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:22.820
and assign contributions by
each effort towards the outcome,

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:27.025
and do so in a way that doesn't
double count or overattribute--

00:20:27.025 --> 00:20:28.150
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:30.390
DAN NEGREY: --or
anything like that.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:30.890
Great.

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:35.990
Well, we had both of those
definitions there in the chat.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:39.320
Sirdark-- and apologies if
I'm pronouncing your name

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:39.920
incorrectly--

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:44.557
I see you had a follow-up
question on MMM.

00:20:44.557 --> 00:20:45.390
You want to jump in?

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:52.160
And a question-- let
me just scroll up.

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:56.900
The question was, "What are
the shortcomings of MMM?"

00:20:56.900 --> 00:21:02.900
or, as we say, Marketing Mix
Models or Mix Media Models?

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:04.850
Good question, and I'm
going to preface this

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:11.050
by saying that I've worn
a lot of hats in 16 or 17

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:14.920
years of doing work like this
and definitely more on the--

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:16.990
I'm more interested
in the technical side

00:21:16.990 --> 00:21:20.870
of implementing the solutions
that we're building.

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:24.052
But I can definitely
get anyone in touch

00:21:24.052 --> 00:21:25.510
with some of our
colleagues who are

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:29.620
more familiar with the
statistical methodology

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:34.320
and the pros and cons there,
but I'll do my best to answer.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:38.900
I think in some cases--

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:43.780
one thing I'll say is that
you need historical data,

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:45.820
or you need a great
way to simulate

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:47.530
what you think is a
good representation

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.878
of historical data
that doesn't exist.

00:21:49.878 --> 00:21:52.420
And then that's the case with
a lot of-- with pretty much any

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:57.580
model you build, but in some
cases, for marketing data,

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:01.690
a lot of times, that data
doesn't exist or isn't in--

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:05.710
you might have data that's--

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:08.800
Well, one thing I'll
say is, as marketing

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:13.240
is more digital nowadays
and is being executed

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:15.490
through all sorts of
different social media

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:18.550
platforms or other
online platforms,

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:20.440
what you might be
able to extract out

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:24.130
of those platforms may not be
in the best form for a model.

00:22:24.130 --> 00:22:26.640
It might not be in the most
ideal form that you'd want,

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:27.220
you know?

00:22:27.220 --> 00:22:31.990
Let's say you have new
customer signups by day--

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:34.120
just some generic term, right?

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:40.240
But maybe you can only get
Facebook advertising spend

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:42.110
at a week level or something.

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:46.950
So there's assumptions
you can make

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:48.330
about spreading
some of that data

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:50.490
around to try and get it
to fit the structure you

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:53.240
need for building a model.

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:57.777
So I would say that, in terms of
pros and cons, it's dependent--

00:22:57.777 --> 00:22:59.610
if you like data munging,
it's a great space

00:22:59.610 --> 00:23:01.202
to be in because you
get data from all

00:23:01.202 --> 00:23:03.660
these disparate systems, and
it's all in different formats,

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:06.810
and your job is to
blend it in a way that

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:09.900
makes logical sense for
explaining the problem

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:12.680
that you're working on.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:15.180
The pros, I'll say--

00:23:15.180 --> 00:23:16.860
and this is the
most obvious one--

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:19.980
is that you can have a
conversation with the CMO

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:24.090
and tell them that you've got a
sound statistical approach that

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:29.140
suggests channel
x has contributed

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:31.180
y percent towards this
particular outcome,

00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:33.400
and a different channel
has contributed this.

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:35.860
And had you done
this instead, this

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:37.720
might have been the
outcome, and you

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:41.680
can test that going forward and
over the next quarter or six

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:43.670
months or whatever.

00:23:43.670 --> 00:23:47.320
And a lot of times, that's
eye-opening for a CMO.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:50.020
There's a lot of pressure, I
think, on CMOs, Chief Marketing

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:52.275
Officers, these days to show--

00:23:52.275 --> 00:23:53.650
they spend a lot
of money, right?

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:55.420
So there's a lot
of pressure to show

00:23:55.420 --> 00:23:57.220
the value of all that spend.

00:23:57.220 --> 00:24:02.620
And if every channel that
you execute stuff through

00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:05.830
is probably going to offer some
kind of reporting attribution

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:08.080
that only looks at what
they do and only considers

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.030
it being the only
thing that somebody

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:12.070
might have been exposed to.

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:15.310
And, obviously, that's not
really practical, right?

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:18.970
So if you've got a tool that can
consider all the things you're

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:22.210
doing, where the spend all
adds up to your total budget,

00:24:22.210 --> 00:24:24.280
and the outcomes
all add up to what

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:27.640
was observed by the whole
organization, and you can say,

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:31.840
based on our model, we think
that Facebook did this,

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.520
or Google did this, or
direct mail did this.

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:40.390
And so now that CMO can have
a much better conversation

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:44.320
with the CFO and the rest of the
organization about how they can

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:46.870
optimize that
budget to keep costs

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:50.890
under control while trying
to maximize the outcome--

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:54.100
new signups, new customers,
revenue, that sort of thing.

00:24:56.645 --> 00:24:57.270
That's great.

00:24:57.270 --> 00:25:00.490
Thank you.

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:04.270
Dan, I'd love to hear a
little bit about your journey

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:05.560
into your role now.

00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:09.250
And I know you previously were
a analytics consultant as well.

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:12.880
What has the transition
been like into director

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:14.540
of analytics role?

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:15.430
Sure, sure, sure.

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:15.930
No problem.

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:18.520
I'll probably go back a
little bit more in time.

00:25:18.520 --> 00:25:20.020
So I was going to
be a math teacher.

00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:21.970
That was the start
of my journey.

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:25.030
So I got a bachelor's degree
in secondary math education

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:29.380
from Ohio University and,
towards the end of that,

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:33.160
decided I wanted to just keep
learning more math and stats.

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:36.937
I wasn't sure if I wanted to get
into public school education,

00:25:36.937 --> 00:25:39.020
or at least wanted to put
it off for a little bit.

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:41.478
So I had an opportunity to stay
at OU and go to grad school

00:25:41.478 --> 00:25:44.080
and study applied
math and statistics.

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:49.002
And right around that
time is when jobs in--

00:25:49.002 --> 00:25:50.710
they weren't calling
it data science yet,

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:54.207
but it was definitely more
statistician, or maybe

00:25:54.207 --> 00:25:55.540
if you're lucky, analytics jobs.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:57.910
This was, like, 2006-ish.

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:03.340
And so, yeah, I thought this
stuff was super interesting.

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:06.520
And it wasn't like
actual science,

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:08.470
not to say that that's
not interesting,

00:26:08.470 --> 00:26:12.160
but just the idea that there's
this emerging field of--

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:13.480
it's not fleshed out yet.

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:16.690
You're kind of using a little
bit of computer science,

00:26:16.690 --> 00:26:19.900
a little bit of math and
stats, and just some business,

00:26:19.900 --> 00:26:23.830
or otherwise context acumen
to solve interesting problems.

00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:28.390
And so when I finished school,
when I finished grad school,

00:26:28.390 --> 00:26:32.050
I had a couple of different
jobs for a year or so,

00:26:32.050 --> 00:26:33.160
each at larger companies.

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:36.520
And then had an
opportunity to work

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:39.490
for a locally owned consulting
company in Cleveland.

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:41.980
And that's really where
I spent a lot of time,

00:26:41.980 --> 00:26:45.100
almost nine years
altogether, but went

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:49.060
from a consultant with a
few years of experience all

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:50.350
the way up through--

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:52.420
by the time I left
there, I was a director

00:26:52.420 --> 00:26:56.050
and had been leading projects
and leading longer term

00:26:56.050 --> 00:26:59.510
engagements, but also working
on R&amp;amp;D side with new technology.

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:03.610
So that's what really got me
interested in this stuff was

00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:09.270
they trusted me to work on
helping our company grow

00:27:09.270 --> 00:27:12.005
into using this--

00:27:12.005 --> 00:27:13.380
I'll go back,
like, 10 years ago.

00:27:13.380 --> 00:27:16.095
There was just an explosion
of open source technology.

00:27:16.095 --> 00:27:17.970
Like, there was a lot
of proprietary software

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:19.920
that was getting to
be very expensive,

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:23.940
and it became very well known
that tools like R or Python

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:28.290
were starting to become used
in more business contexts.

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:30.907
And, also, at the same time,
the argument used to be,

00:27:30.907 --> 00:27:33.240
oh, well, it's in-memory
computing, and what can you do?

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:36.300
Like, computer has 2
gigs of RAM or whatever.

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:39.780
Well, then that price of
memory started going down,

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:43.500
and they started being able
to spin up virtual machines

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:48.380
and keep an eye on that
cost a little bit more.

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:51.890
So that's when we started,
and this is all just coming--

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:54.170
this is so interesting
of the timing of all this

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:57.320
because right around
the same time, it was,

00:27:57.320 --> 00:28:00.950
like, RStudio was
getting bigger,

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:04.310
and there was more awareness
of the packages coming out.

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:07.550
Ggplot2 had already been pretty
big, I think, at the time,

00:28:07.550 --> 00:28:09.303
but then those HTML
widgets came out.

00:28:09.303 --> 00:28:10.970
And it's like, oh,
look what you can do.

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:15.080
You can embed an interactive
map or a table or something

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:17.990
in what looks to be a
presentation or a document

00:28:17.990 --> 00:28:20.660
or something.

00:28:20.660 --> 00:28:22.935
And this is totally the
section of this hour-long chat

00:28:22.935 --> 00:28:23.810
where I could ramble.

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:26.930
So if you want to change gears,
Rachael, you can stop me, but--

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:28.070
DAN NEGREY: --I love
talking about this stuff.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:28.737
This is great.

00:28:28.737 --> 00:28:33.920
We have a lot of former
teachers on here, actually,

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:37.140
as well as wannabe math teachers
who are commenting in the chat

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:37.640
too.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:38.140
[LAUGHS]

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:39.740
Well, I should mention that--

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:40.670
it's never left.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:43.260
Like, I did never end up
teaching in a public school,

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:45.920
but I did teach undergrad
math in grad school.

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:48.080
And every job I've
had, I've taken it

00:28:48.080 --> 00:28:52.470
as an unspoken responsibility
to train and teach people.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:55.070
Especially as this stuff
has just exploded, there's--

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:57.260
I mean, if you're new, if
you're finishing school,

00:28:57.260 --> 00:28:59.870
and if you've not had exposure
to a lot of this stuff,

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:01.880
you probably think,
where do I start?

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:05.960
These companies expect me to
be a brilliant statistician

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:08.720
and have 10 years of
computer science background

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:11.760
and all this kind of stuff,
and it's not practical.

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:14.360
So there needs to be good
training programs in place

00:29:14.360 --> 00:29:18.200
at places and people patient and
excited about teaching others

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:19.340
how to use it.

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:21.860
But, yeah, so I
had an opportunity

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:25.350
to lead R&amp;amp;D efforts at the one
consulting company I was at

00:29:25.350 --> 00:29:30.710
and get us up to speed on Hadoop
and R and Python and Spark

00:29:30.710 --> 00:29:32.980
and all this cool stuff.

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:36.290
And then, at some
point, I decided

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:38.810
it was time to move on
to a different role,

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:40.655
and I took a role at
a software company.

00:29:40.655 --> 00:29:42.530
That's where Rachael
and I actually first met

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:45.350
because I was trying to get
RStudio Pro products installed

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:46.850
there.

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:49.590
And that was a great experience.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:52.010
I learned a ton about B2B
software and the sales

00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:54.410
and marketing process.

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:57.230
If you've only worked on--
like in a B2C environment,

00:29:57.230 --> 00:30:00.020
working in a B2B environment
is much different in how

00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:03.800
long everything takes and
the marketing efforts that

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:08.150
go into what could be a
year- or two-long sales

00:30:08.150 --> 00:30:10.790
cycle with much bigger
price tag at the end,

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:12.240
so it's an interesting
experience.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:16.670
But after a while, I wanted to
get back into just being more

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:19.610
hands-on,
data-science-developer-type,

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:24.440
but had accumulated a lot of
this experience that I could

00:30:24.440 --> 00:30:26.375
reliably say that I'm--

00:30:26.375 --> 00:30:27.890
call myself a leader
in this space,

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:29.820
even though I still
have imposter syndrome.

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.153
I'm looking at all of you,
and I'm immediately thinking,

00:30:32.153 --> 00:30:34.010
I'm out of my league
here, and you guys

00:30:34.010 --> 00:30:36.560
should be the ones talking.

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:41.630
So I met people at
MarketBridge, and I

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:43.770
knew that we clicked
in terms of our vision.

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:45.020
It was also a smaller company.

00:30:45.020 --> 00:30:47.780
I think I just have a--

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:50.750
my preference is to just work
in a smaller company that

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:52.940
can move a little
bit faster and not

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:57.080
have as much red tape across
when it comes to trying things

00:30:57.080 --> 00:30:59.960
out with technology.

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:06.180
So, yeah, so now we're here,
and I've kind of taken--

00:31:06.180 --> 00:31:07.650
everything is cumulative.

00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:10.330
Everything I've done
for the last 15 years,

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:13.800
I feel like I've tried
to put into play here.

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:16.830
And it's teaching others.

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:18.660
It's building
internal packages so

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:21.330
that we can abstract
problems and have

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:26.790
people use consistent functions
to address similar problems.

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:29.610
It's Shiny.

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:30.400
It's everything.

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:31.410
I mean, it's great.

00:31:31.410 --> 00:31:35.983
It's exploring new ways of
data engineering that don't--

00:31:35.983 --> 00:31:37.650
I still love a good
relational database,

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:40.200
but there's use cases
beyond that these days that

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:47.423
are requiring more just
cloud-based tools that are just

00:31:47.423 --> 00:31:49.090
going in different
directions and trying

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:52.930
to keep a handle on all
these different things

00:31:52.930 --> 00:31:55.220
and how they fit together.

00:31:55.220 --> 00:31:58.480
I think that's another
good point, too, is like--

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:02.610
and, actually, if you've
ever been in a sales pitch

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:07.663
or in a meeting where
that's been sort of pitchy

00:32:07.663 --> 00:32:09.330
with sales and
marketing, if you've ever

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:11.640
seen a slide that's just
got logos blasted on it,

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:13.015
I feel like that,
sometimes, when

00:32:13.015 --> 00:32:14.860
you talk about data
science and technology,

00:32:14.860 --> 00:32:16.860
you could get one of
those, and there could just

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:18.510
be every tool in the
world you think about.

00:32:18.510 --> 00:32:20.427
So again, it's one of
those things of thinking

00:32:20.427 --> 00:32:23.120
about what does what?

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:25.020
How does it fit together, right?

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:29.340
Like, it's amazing that you
can do a ton of stuff in R,

00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:30.630
and I literally mean a ton.

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:32.370
But it can be even
cooler when you

00:32:32.370 --> 00:32:38.660
think about how it can work
with other software or hardware,

00:32:38.660 --> 00:32:39.363
for example.

00:32:39.363 --> 00:32:40.280
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Yeah.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:41.360
DAN NEGREY: I'll
give a plug for pins.

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:42.985
Pins has been a huge
package that we've

00:32:42.985 --> 00:32:46.610
started using a year or so ago.

00:32:46.610 --> 00:32:51.560
And we've just got
into using Azure blob

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:54.740
containers as the boards
for pinning objects

00:32:54.740 --> 00:32:55.470
and reading from.

00:32:55.470 --> 00:32:58.610
So if you've never
used pins, it's

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:00.140
definitely worth
checking out, even

00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:05.353
if you're using local folders
as the backend boards.

00:33:05.353 --> 00:33:06.770
Just the idea of
abstraction there

00:33:06.770 --> 00:33:10.073
is really cool in what
you can do with it.

00:33:10.073 --> 00:33:11.240
I love the pins shout-out.

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:13.220
I put the link to
it in the chat.

00:33:13.220 --> 00:33:15.410
I was trying to
grab an example too

00:33:15.410 --> 00:33:18.810
that shows a whole data science
workflow with pins as well,

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:22.470
and I'll put it into
the chat when I find it.

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:24.213
But, Dan, I was going to say--

00:33:24.213 --> 00:33:25.880
well, thank you for
sharing the journey,

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:30.750
but also, so many of us feel
imposter syndrome here as well,

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:34.310
but we're all here
getting over that.

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:35.600
But this is awesome.

00:33:35.600 --> 00:33:37.250
Thank you so much.

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:40.520
There are a few anonymous
questions that came through,

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:43.880
and one was, "Are there
any specific packages

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:47.720
that you find are good for
manipulating and displaying

00:33:47.720 --> 00:33:52.740
time series data or
packages for missing data?"

00:33:52.740 --> 00:33:54.880
Sure.

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:57.690
So this is interesting
so because I

00:33:57.690 --> 00:34:02.050
started learning R before
the tidyverse, if you ever

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:05.530
looked at my code, you're going
to see a nice blend of bracket

00:34:05.530 --> 00:34:07.870
subsetting meets dplyr
meets all sorts of stuff.

00:34:07.870 --> 00:34:12.159
But I believe it's
sound, and it could

00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:14.780
be totally different than
somebody else's approach.

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:16.790
So sometimes--
this is probably--

00:34:16.790 --> 00:34:18.040
this could be a fault of mine.

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:20.530
Sometimes I probably
code things up

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:22.270
the long way because
it's immediately

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:24.812
the solution that comes to mind
when there's probably already

00:34:24.812 --> 00:34:28.757
a package that does
it in a simple call.

00:34:28.757 --> 00:34:30.090
But that's the fun of it, right?

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:32.797
That's the fun is solving
problems and thinking about how

00:34:32.797 --> 00:34:34.380
it should be solved
and then coding it

00:34:34.380 --> 00:34:36.790
up yourself, even
if at first glance,

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:39.090
it's not the most efficient way.

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:41.080
So dplyr is just incredible.

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:43.020
I remember being at--

00:34:43.020 --> 00:34:46.080
I'm trying to remember exactly
what it was, if it was a useR!

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:47.489
Conference.

00:34:47.489 --> 00:34:49.530
I think it was a useR!

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:53.070
conference at UCLA
in 2014 or something,

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:57.048
and they did a talk about dplyr,
and I thought it was a game

00:34:57.048 --> 00:34:58.590
changer because now,
all of a sudden,

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:03.930
what I found is people had hard
time figuring out manipulating

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:07.680
data in R with the apply()
functions and bracket

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:10.860
subsetting and that sort of
thing when they were used

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:13.500
to an approach that was
more like SQL or SaaS.

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:17.610
So dplyr comes along, and it
sort of speaks to them on how--

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:21.070
gives them a new toolset
for manipulating data.

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:27.720
So as far as time series stuff
goes, I know, for a while,

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:34.140
I was using xts and dygraphs to
get more of formal time series

00:35:34.140 --> 00:35:36.330
objects and visualize those.

00:35:36.330 --> 00:35:39.240
Dygraphs was
incredible with its--

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:41.520
I mean, I'm blown away
when I saw the sliders.

00:35:41.520 --> 00:35:43.950
And if you're not
familiar with it,

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:48.930
you imagine a time series plot
that, right out of the box,

00:35:48.930 --> 00:35:51.480
has sliders at the bottom
that you can easily

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:54.360
pinch in and pull apart and
see different parts of the time

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:56.940
and hover-overs and everything.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:36:00.240
I started using Plotly
a ton for visualization

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:04.750
just because it can do so
much besides time series.

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:06.430
So I just find it--

00:36:06.430 --> 00:36:08.280
got used to using
their framework

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:11.250
for handling a lot of different
visualization problems.

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:15.920
Thank you.

00:36:15.920 --> 00:36:17.590
So going back to pins
for just a second

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:20.830
because I just was reading
Libby's comment in the chat--

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:25.030
if somebody was just getting
started with using pins now,

00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:28.630
is there a specific
resource you'd recommend?

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:31.390
Yeah, I thought
pins.rstudio.com is--

00:36:31.390 --> 00:36:33.760
I still pull that up in
my browser all the time

00:36:33.760 --> 00:36:37.065
and even read through the first
page or the couple of articles

00:36:37.065 --> 00:36:38.065
that are embedded there.

00:36:40.810 --> 00:36:42.880
It does a great job
of explaining it,

00:36:42.880 --> 00:36:44.800
especially, I
think, the examples

00:36:44.800 --> 00:36:48.850
start with just
using a folder right

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:52.850
near your working directory
that can be used to pin stuff.

00:36:52.850 --> 00:36:56.030
And then once you see
what it's doing there,

00:36:56.030 --> 00:36:59.800
and then you look at what
all the other backend storage

00:36:59.800 --> 00:37:01.990
options, it's--

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:03.860
sometimes you've got
to deal with like,

00:37:03.860 --> 00:37:06.995
OK, well, you don't have to
authenticate to a folder that's

00:37:06.995 --> 00:37:08.620
next to your working
directory, but you

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:10.360
do with an Azure blob storage.

00:37:10.360 --> 00:37:13.420
So connecting to that
board is the hurdle.

00:37:13.420 --> 00:37:17.510
But once you have the
board established,

00:37:17.510 --> 00:37:21.490
then you're past that point,
and it can be really cool.

00:37:21.490 --> 00:37:23.702
So start with the
basic examples.

00:37:23.702 --> 00:37:24.202
Yeah.

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:28.950
Perfect.

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:31.110
I just put the link
here because I realized

00:37:31.110 --> 00:37:33.420
the one I shared before
was only pins for R,

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:36.330
but there's also
pins for Python too.

00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:39.810
Eric, I see you were putting
a plug for a package as well.

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:40.800
Do you want to jump in?

00:37:43.490 --> 00:37:44.180
Hello.

00:37:44.180 --> 00:37:45.920
Yeah, this has been
a wonderful talk,

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:49.280
and I really enjoyed learning
from your perspective.

00:37:49.280 --> 00:37:52.040
Just on the topic of
additional packages,

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:53.690
first, yeah, I
love pins as well.

00:37:53.690 --> 00:37:56.525
I've been using that in a lot
of my daily new workflows.

00:37:56.525 --> 00:37:58.400
It's been making things
a lot more efficient.

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:01.100
But with interactive
visualizations,

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:03.590
I've been on a huge
journey of that as well.

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:05.390
I'll give a nice
