tools.

00:39:15.140 --> 00:39:16.557
We'll just do
everything in Shiny.

00:39:16.557 --> 00:39:20.243
And a lot of times, I
have taken that stance,

00:39:20.243 --> 00:39:21.410
and it doesn't go very well.

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:27.560
But I think those
tools have a good--

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:29.750
they serve a good
purpose in terms

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:36.490
of keeping an eye on a lot
of those KPIs and things

00:39:36.490 --> 00:39:37.840
I measured before.

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:41.250
I think there's a good
audience for those tools,

00:39:41.250 --> 00:39:44.280
and they're a great way to
learn data visualization

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:45.220
and manipulation.

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:47.745
I think where I've--

00:39:47.745 --> 00:39:51.720
I guess one thing I'll say
about those is it's not

00:39:51.720 --> 00:39:54.390
worth fighting about BI tools.

00:39:54.390 --> 00:39:57.030
Like, you shouldn't probably
go into a conversation,

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:58.980
Shiny should replace this.

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:00.480
There's a lot of
things you'll find.

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:03.630
Even if the leadership
supports that, you'll find

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:06.810
there's a lot of
things that IT support

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:09.120
and human resources that
can do all the work,

00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:11.020
and all that kind of
stuff comes into play.

00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:14.370
So the conversation I
found as more helpful

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:17.790
is when you can demonstrate
what something like Shiny

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:21.780
can do that those tools can't
or the use case for those,

00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:25.540
or serving up model scores
through plumber API and RStudio

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:26.040
Connect.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:27.540
Like, that's a good
example of stuff

00:40:27.540 --> 00:40:30.600
that's probably much
more difficult or maybe

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:33.570
even impossible to do in
some enterprise BI tools.

00:40:33.570 --> 00:40:36.480
So try to focus on the
use cases that those tools

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:44.532
can help solve instead of coming
in with your sleeves rolled up.

00:40:44.532 --> 00:40:45.490
That's a great point.

00:40:45.490 --> 00:40:46.620
Thank you.

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:50.010
Oh, I will say, too,
there's some of the--

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:51.810
actually, just this
just came up recently

00:40:51.810 --> 00:40:55.350
about integrating
Tableau with R.

00:40:55.350 --> 00:40:58.620
And I had heard about the
Rserve integration a while ago,

00:40:58.620 --> 00:41:00.900
and then explored it once
to see that it could work,

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:03.112
and then hadn't done
anything with it.

00:41:03.112 --> 00:41:06.110
But we might have a use case
where we're doing something

00:41:06.110 --> 00:41:06.610
with that.

00:41:06.610 --> 00:41:12.223
So that's another--
it's fun to think

00:41:12.223 --> 00:41:14.640
about the different roles you
can play in the type of work

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:15.900
we do, right?

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:18.300
And that's what I think is
cool about this type of work.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:20.970
Like, sometimes, I could put my
hat on and just be a modeler,

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:22.620
and I don't care about
the prettiest visuals.

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:23.700
I'm trying to get a good model.

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:25.530
And sometimes, I could
be a data engineer,

00:41:25.530 --> 00:41:29.520
and I'm trying to create
the most reproducible

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:32.910
or easy-to-follow pipeline
for moving data around.

00:41:32.910 --> 00:41:36.390
And sometimes, I could
put my artistic hat on

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:38.310
and be trying to create
the most aesthetically

00:41:38.310 --> 00:41:41.233
pleasing dashboard out there.

00:41:41.233 --> 00:41:42.900
And I think it can
be kind of cool, too,

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:44.310
when you think that there's--

00:41:44.310 --> 00:41:46.852
I think it goes back to what I
said about other technologies.

00:41:46.852 --> 00:41:50.610
I would say if somebody else
has the role on the presentation

00:41:50.610 --> 00:41:52.770
layer, we'll say, then
let them own that role,

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:57.300
and try to do what you can do
to get your stuff integrated

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:58.740
with that.

00:41:58.740 --> 00:42:02.310
It could be a shared product
between the two of you.

00:42:05.260 --> 00:42:08.950
Alan I see you had a question
on a project lifecycle.

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:10.210
Do you want to jump in?

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:11.740
Yeah, sure.

00:42:11.740 --> 00:42:16.180
I'm curious again if there's
a kind of typical project

00:42:16.180 --> 00:42:18.160
or if there's just
a ton of variation.

00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:22.030
And going back to the root
of the question, I think,

00:42:22.030 --> 00:42:26.500
is around like, do you end up
making robust infrastructure

00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:31.180
for a customer that needs
an enduring way to collect

00:42:31.180 --> 00:42:34.360
data that produces some
kind of output for them

00:42:34.360 --> 00:42:36.564
that then they're monitoring?

00:42:36.564 --> 00:42:39.400
And/or do they come in with a
bunch of data, and they say,

00:42:39.400 --> 00:42:42.550
we need an analysis and a
way to answer this question

00:42:42.550 --> 00:42:44.920
about market value?

00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:47.950
Could both really point in terms
of different directions of how

00:42:47.950 --> 00:42:49.602
you support what you make?

00:42:49.602 --> 00:42:50.310
DAN NEGREY: Yeah.

00:42:50.310 --> 00:42:52.685
And so I'm wondering if
there's one place you generally

00:42:52.685 --> 00:42:55.870
land, or if every project
is kind of a consultative

00:42:55.870 --> 00:42:59.710
endeavor, and, ultimately,
if it means-- like,

00:42:59.710 --> 00:43:02.500
do you have a single
productized thing?

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:05.370
Or are you all over the map?

00:43:05.370 --> 00:43:06.790
Yeah, a good question.

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:12.590
I would say the first part,
more the latter of clients

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:17.420
coming with their data so that
we can build models and reports

00:43:17.420 --> 00:43:20.030
and analytics off of that.

00:43:20.030 --> 00:43:22.140
In other cases, we're
in their environments

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:26.630
and having to do a lot
of the detective work

00:43:26.630 --> 00:43:28.940
to figure out where data
is and how to get it.

00:43:28.940 --> 00:43:31.010
And then in other
cases, finding out

00:43:31.010 --> 00:43:33.110
that data still resides
in source systems,

00:43:33.110 --> 00:43:36.703
and so we'll be in the
client environment--

00:43:36.703 --> 00:43:38.120
I mean, this is
just another case.

00:43:38.120 --> 00:43:41.073
So we might be the ones to
hook into that source system

00:43:41.073 --> 00:43:42.740
and get it to our
environment, so we can

00:43:42.740 --> 00:43:44.480
leverage our compute resources.

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:46.132
Or they might not
be cool with that,

00:43:46.132 --> 00:43:47.840
and we have to stay
in their environment,

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:49.798
but we're still hooking
into the source system.

00:43:49.798 --> 00:43:53.090
We've had situations where we've
built databases from the ground

00:43:53.090 --> 00:43:56.180
up inside a client environment
so that we can ingest data

00:43:56.180 --> 00:43:57.830
from some of those
systems to support

00:43:57.830 --> 00:44:02.040
the different type of
analytics work we do.

00:44:02.040 --> 00:44:04.250
So kind of all over
the map, really, with--

00:44:07.232 --> 00:44:09.440
definitely have a preference
to what we'd want to do,

00:44:09.440 --> 00:44:12.920
but in a lot of cases,
especially dealing with data,

00:44:12.920 --> 00:44:14.870
there's compliance
and governance issues

00:44:14.870 --> 00:44:16.850
and constraints
that maybe suggest

00:44:16.850 --> 00:44:19.550
the type of environment
stuff can go or should go,

00:44:19.550 --> 00:44:23.420
and we deal with a lot of that.

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:27.070
So a lot of variation and
a lot of customer-by-customer

00:44:27.070 --> 00:44:28.150
problem solving.

00:44:28.150 --> 00:44:30.130
Yeah, for the most part.

00:44:30.130 --> 00:44:32.760
ALAN SCHUSSMAN: Cool, thank you.

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:34.880
Jared, I realize we
missed one of your questions

00:44:34.880 --> 00:44:36.413
from a bit earlier.

00:44:36.413 --> 00:44:37.580
Do you want to jump in here?

00:44:42.350 --> 00:44:44.733
Oh, I don't know if
we can hear you yet.

00:44:44.733 --> 00:44:45.400
Can you hear us?

00:44:45.400 --> 00:44:45.950
JARED WOOLFOLK: Oh no.

00:44:45.950 --> 00:44:46.610
Can you guys hear me OK?

00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:47.693
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Oh, nope.

00:44:47.693 --> 00:44:48.510
Yes, yes, we can.

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:51.135
JARED WOOLFOLK: Yeah, I guess my
question is more generalistic.

00:44:51.135 --> 00:44:54.560
But handling client
ambiguity when

00:44:54.560 --> 00:44:59.750
they come to you with a need,
if you have any tips, tricks

00:44:59.750 --> 00:45:05.000
or insight or wisdom on shoring
up and getting more specifics

00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:09.530
instead of more having
an aimless ship trying

00:45:09.530 --> 00:45:13.770
to reach a destination
on reaching

00:45:13.770 --> 00:45:16.660
a final result or a final
product that they are after

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:19.950
and how you've kind of handled
that through your career.

00:45:19.950 --> 00:45:22.190
Sure, sure.

00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:24.710
One thing I've seen
that's been helpful

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:28.820
is when engaging with a new
organization, new client,

00:45:28.820 --> 00:45:32.780
is whether you show them this
or not is having a maturity

00:45:32.780 --> 00:45:35.030
framework just so you
can assess where they're

00:45:35.030 --> 00:45:38.750
at in terms of
data accessibility,

00:45:38.750 --> 00:45:41.390
the types of analytics
or models or insights

00:45:41.390 --> 00:45:44.210
they currently develop,
and the tools they use,

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:46.640
so get an understanding
of their environment.

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:50.150
If you plot all that out,
the first thing you might say

00:45:50.150 --> 00:45:52.550
is that they're pretty low on--

00:45:52.550 --> 00:45:54.620
they don't have hardly
any of this stuff.

00:45:54.620 --> 00:45:56.480
So maybe the first thing--

00:45:56.480 --> 00:45:58.700
And I hear what you're
saying about ambiguity.

00:45:58.700 --> 00:46:01.040
They might have
that type of setup,

00:46:01.040 --> 00:46:02.540
but then they might
also come to you

00:46:02.540 --> 00:46:03.830
with very specific questions.

00:46:03.830 --> 00:46:05.997
And you think, well, before
we can even get to this,

00:46:05.997 --> 00:46:09.830
we have to set up
this foundation.

00:46:09.830 --> 00:46:14.000
And if you can talk
to them about that,

00:46:14.000 --> 00:46:17.070
maybe building that foundation
is a place you can start.

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:19.710
If they're not patient
enough for that,

00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:22.400
you can get into
maybe more specifics

00:46:22.400 --> 00:46:25.910
around the data that's needed
to answer the specific questions

00:46:25.910 --> 00:46:28.130
they have.

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:30.260
I think a lot of times,
especially when you're

00:46:30.260 --> 00:46:31.430
dealing with
leadership, they don't

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:33.347
know that that gap exists
between what they're

00:46:33.347 --> 00:46:35.510
looking for and the
resources and tools they

00:46:35.510 --> 00:46:37.200
have in place to achieve that.

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:38.960
So if you can
bring a perspective

00:46:38.960 --> 00:46:42.500
on what you can do to
help them achieve that,

00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:45.630
that's always huge.

00:46:45.630 --> 00:46:53.540
And then just as far as other
ambiguity with questions,

00:46:53.540 --> 00:46:56.220
I think that's come
with experience to--

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:58.910
this is where the context
and the business acumen

00:46:58.910 --> 00:47:00.062
comes into play, right?

00:47:00.062 --> 00:47:00.770
I say "business."

00:47:00.770 --> 00:47:01.820
I like the word "context."

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:03.487
I don't like the word
"business acumen,"

00:47:03.487 --> 00:47:05.210
but context of the
problem because you

00:47:05.210 --> 00:47:08.120
could be working
for pharmaceuticals,

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:11.770
or you can be working
for local government.

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:13.640
The more you know
about the context,

00:47:13.640 --> 00:47:17.500
the more you are aware of
the pitfalls that people

00:47:17.500 --> 00:47:20.020
in decision-making roles--

00:47:20.020 --> 00:47:22.240
that they'll run
into, and the more

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:25.730
you can anticipate situations
that they might encounter,

00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:27.730
and you could do some
work to help support that.

00:47:33.510 --> 00:47:37.255
Anyone that has maybe no coding
abilities but tons of contacts,

00:47:37.255 --> 00:47:39.630
they're a valuable person to
have around because they can

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:41.280
help shape those conversations.

00:47:43.498 --> 00:47:44.665
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Absolutely.

00:47:44.665 --> 00:47:45.700
JARED WOOLFOLK: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:47:45.700 --> 00:47:46.460
Yeah.

00:47:46.460 --> 00:47:46.960
Thanks.

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:47.560
Thanks, Dan.

