I was floored, and I
got really into that.

00:51:35.965 --> 00:51:38.590
There's a book called The Linux
Command Line by William Shotts,

00:51:38.590 --> 00:51:41.630
and it's available
free as a PDF.

00:51:41.630 --> 00:51:44.530
It's not too expensive
on Amazon either.

00:51:44.530 --> 00:51:47.380
If you're interested in learning
more about Linux servers

00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:50.360
and command-line tools,
it's a great introduction,

00:51:50.360 --> 00:51:52.510
especially if you're not
familiar with that stuff

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:53.020
already--

00:51:53.020 --> 00:51:55.360
kind of eases you, kind
of relates everything back

00:51:55.360 --> 00:51:58.210
to some of the more GUI OS
stuff that you might already

00:51:58.210 --> 00:52:00.630
be familiar with.

00:52:00.630 --> 00:52:02.260
Thank you.

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:04.470
I see there's a
conversation happening

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:07.110
in the chat around,
I think, Sneehall,

00:52:07.110 --> 00:52:09.450
you had a question on
any book recommendations

00:52:09.450 --> 00:52:15.210
for Power BI or Power
BI with R. Does anybody

00:52:15.210 --> 00:52:20.136
who put some recommendations in
there want to jump in as well?

00:52:20.136 --> 00:52:21.840
Or, Josiah, I see
you're commenting

00:52:21.840 --> 00:52:26.183
on using REST APIs there.

00:52:26.183 --> 00:52:27.850
I don't want to put
anybody on the spot.

00:52:27.850 --> 00:52:31.870
I just see it was an engaged
conversation happening.

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:32.810
I can comment on it.

00:52:32.810 --> 00:52:35.350
I don't want to steal
anyone's thunder.

00:52:35.350 --> 00:52:37.020
Sure, go ahead.

00:52:37.020 --> 00:52:38.373
Not stealing anyone's thunder.

00:52:38.373 --> 00:52:39.790
Feel free to raise
your hand, too,

00:52:39.790 --> 00:52:41.165
if anybody wants
to add anything.

00:52:44.050 --> 00:52:47.650
I'm a big advocate
of REST APIs.

00:52:47.650 --> 00:52:49.810
Any software can
access a REST API.

00:52:49.810 --> 00:52:52.720
So Power BI can
access a REST API.

00:52:52.720 --> 00:52:56.130
Tableau can access a REST
API, and anything can.

00:52:56.130 --> 00:52:58.330
So the idea is
that you can make--

00:52:58.330 --> 00:53:01.390
if you can run a function in
R, you can make a REST API.

00:53:01.390 --> 00:53:03.080
So the question
just then becomes,

00:53:03.080 --> 00:53:04.390
how do you host that REST API?

00:53:04.390 --> 00:53:06.458
So maybe you want to
have a function that

00:53:06.458 --> 00:53:07.375
manipulates your data.

00:53:07.375 --> 00:53:10.330
If you want Power BI to use
that, all you need to do

00:53:10.330 --> 00:53:13.360
is make it accessible
via API and then Power--

00:53:13.360 --> 00:53:17.350
I just found like 10 media
posts on this just by one Google

00:53:17.350 --> 00:53:21.460
search, how to connect
Power BI to REST API.

00:53:21.460 --> 00:53:23.000
That's simple.

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:28.215
The question is, how do
you host this REST API?

00:53:28.215 --> 00:53:30.340
And then making those two
things talk to each other

00:53:30.340 --> 00:53:31.995
is probably quite trivial.

00:53:31.995 --> 00:53:33.370
The hosting part's
the hard part,

00:53:33.370 --> 00:53:35.530
but I'm sure there's 25
people in this chat who

00:53:35.530 --> 00:53:37.697
can help you figure out how
to do that with Connect.

00:53:37.697 --> 00:53:39.765
[LAUGHS] But I think
all I want to say

00:53:39.765 --> 00:53:41.140
is that you can
write a function.

00:53:41.140 --> 00:53:44.510
You can make a REST API.

00:53:44.510 --> 00:53:45.210
Awesome.

00:53:45.210 --> 00:53:46.870
Thanks for that.

00:53:46.870 --> 00:53:48.720
Thank you for all
the recommendations

00:53:48.720 --> 00:53:51.480
there and on other
books too in the chat

00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:56.640
I see Tatsu just put a shout-out
for DevOps for Data Science

00:53:56.640 --> 00:53:58.950
by Alex Gold.

00:53:58.950 --> 00:54:01.590
We should definitely
add that one to the list

00:54:01.590 --> 00:54:05.140
too and capture
some of those there.

00:54:05.140 --> 00:54:09.480
So, Dan, I know we're coming
up quickly on our time here,

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:11.340
but a question I
wanted to ask you--

00:54:11.340 --> 00:54:13.410
and I think we touched
on it a little bit--

00:54:13.410 --> 00:54:15.990
is when you think
of the year ahead

00:54:15.990 --> 00:54:20.100
for your team, what is something
that you're most excited about?

00:54:22.800 --> 00:54:24.900
Oh, good question.

00:54:24.900 --> 00:54:29.190
I think about the work
we're doing with my team

00:54:29.190 --> 00:54:30.570
specifically, with
the work we're

00:54:30.570 --> 00:54:32.620
doing with marketing
effectiveness,

00:54:32.620 --> 00:54:36.390
and I think what excites
me the most about that

00:54:36.390 --> 00:54:39.960
is that we've done a
lot of these projects

00:54:39.960 --> 00:54:43.507
and are approaching it
from, how do we abstract it?

00:54:43.507 --> 00:54:45.090
Like, there's so
many things in common

00:54:45.090 --> 00:54:47.380
from one project to the next.

00:54:47.380 --> 00:54:50.400
And so we're not--

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:53.190
yes, every client
can be different.

00:54:53.190 --> 00:54:56.240
Every outcome could be
different whether, it's

00:54:56.240 --> 00:54:59.010
a count of something or a
spend amount or whatever.

00:54:59.010 --> 00:55:03.210
So we're always thinking about
how these problems relate

00:55:03.210 --> 00:55:05.255
and how they're common
from one to the other

00:55:05.255 --> 00:55:06.630
or how they differ
so that we can

00:55:06.630 --> 00:55:08.880
expand how we abstract those.

00:55:08.880 --> 00:55:10.380
And when you can
abstract a problem,

00:55:10.380 --> 00:55:13.307
you can create reusable
solutions and classes

00:55:13.307 --> 00:55:15.640
and methods, and that's exactly
what we're trying to do.

00:55:15.640 --> 00:55:19.298
So that helps us be more
consistent with the way

00:55:19.298 --> 00:55:20.340
we approach the problems.

00:55:20.340 --> 00:55:24.330
And it's sort of a
self-sustaining model

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:26.490
too because then
we can train people

00:55:26.490 --> 00:55:28.020
on how these things
work, and we can

00:55:28.020 --> 00:55:30.730
reason about them the same way
when we talk to each other.

00:55:30.730 --> 00:55:33.113
It's a very exciting way to
approach this type of work

00:55:33.113 --> 00:55:34.530
that I've wanted
to do for a while

00:55:34.530 --> 00:55:38.430
but just finally have the
opportunity to approach it

00:55:38.430 --> 00:55:40.510
this way with our support
of our leadership.

00:55:40.510 --> 00:55:43.535
So it's really cool.

00:55:43.535 --> 00:55:44.160
That's great.

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:45.690
Thank you, Dan.

00:55:45.690 --> 00:55:47.550
I just wanted to
check that I haven't

00:55:47.550 --> 00:55:52.000
